      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long do Ryobi ONE+ batteries last?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Three to five years and roughly 300 to 500 full charge cycles is typical for a lithium pack treated normally. Occasional DIY users often get five to eight years because they never approach the cycle limit, while daily trade use reaches it in one and a half to three. Storage conditions move this more than usage does."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How long does a Ryobi battery last on one charge?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends entirely on the tool. A 4.0 amp-hour pack runs a work light for a day, drives 600 to 1,000 screws, makes 80 to 120 cuts with a circular saw, or trims for 25 to 35 minutes. High-draw tools like chainsaws and mowers measure runtime in minutes, which is why capacity matters most there."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do Ryobi batteries go bad if not used?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Lithium cells degrade over time regardless of use, a process called calendar ageing, and it runs faster when hot or when the pack is held at full charge. For occasional users this dominates, which means where and how you store packs matters more than how you use them."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best way to store Ryobi batteries?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "At roughly half charge, indoors at room temperature, off the charger, with terminals protected from anything metal. Check them every three months and top up if they have drifted low. A cupboard in the house is meaningfully better than a garage, and anything is better than a vehicle."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Why does my Ryobi battery die so fast now?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Almost certainly lost capacity through ageing, especially if it also charges faster than it used to and sags quickly under load. Increased internal resistance is the mechanism. The pack is not necessarily scrap; it is usually still fine for a work light, an inflator or light drilling."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can you overcharge a Ryobi battery?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Modern chargers stop when the pack is full, so you will not overcharge it in the damaging sense. What you will do by leaving it on indefinitely is hold the cells at 100 percent state of charge, which accelerates degradation over months. Charge, then remove it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it bad to run a Ryobi battery completely flat?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Occasionally, no — the protection circuit cuts off before genuine damage. As a habit it is harder on the cells than stopping when the tool starts fading, and leaving a pack stored in that state is genuinely destructive, because it can drift below the voltage at which the charger will accept it."
          }
        }
